1.3 What this book will teach you to do
By working through the book, you will learn how to:

decide when a change warrants a specification and when a direct edit is enough;
turn an incomplete request into testable requirements and scenarios;
place rationale, behavior, design decisions, and tasks in the appropriate OpenSpec artifacts;
give Copilot focused repository context across sessions and repositories;
compare code and tests with accepted behavior rather than with the latest chat response;
modify accepted requirements, revise work already in progress, and coordinate parallel changes without discarding their history; and
synchronize and archive completed changes so another developer can reconstruct what changed and why.

This workflow can expose ambiguity before code generation, give reviewers an explicit behavioral target, and preserve decisions beyond one Copilot session. OpenSpec does not guarantee better code. It can make intent reviewable and give the team something concrete to verify the implementation against.

1.9 Hands-on exercise
Review the unit-number Jira story and list every behavior GitHub Copilot would otherwise have to guess. Classify each item as one of the following:

a business requirement;
an implementation decision;
a test or verification question; or
an explicit non-goal.

7.10 Compare VS Code and IntelliJ IDEA
Run the same bounded prompt in VS Code and IntelliJ IDEA after both environments can discover the OpenSpec workflow files. Exact wording need not match. Compare whether each response:

inspected both repository evidence and OpenSpec context;
preserved the distinction among stated, observed, inferred, and open material;
cited files that actually support its claims;
found the same implementation-blocking questions; and
avoided creating change artifacts or implementation details.

Differences could point to context discovery rather than model quality. Did one IDE have the correct repository root open? Could both see the same generated prompt files and instructions? Were the same Jira text and repository baseline available? Check those conditions before judging the responses by their prose.
Combine verified findings into one decision record. Two competing records would leave Chapter 8 without a stable source, even if each IDE produced useful observations.

7.11 Know when to stop exploring
More exploration does not always produce more certainty. Repository search cannot answer whether the business wants partial matching, and repeated prompts may only produce increasingly polished guesses. Consider the exploration ready for review when:

every behavior needed for an observable scenario has a decision, named owner, or explicit deferral;
every “Observed” claim has a checked repository reference;
every inference remains labeled as an option;
backend and frontend expectations no longer contradict each other without a recorded owner; and
non-goals define what the first change should leave alone.

The record may still contain unanswered questions, provided each one has an owner or an explicit deferral. Once repository evidence runs out, another search may add less than a short conversation with the product owner or API lead.

11.5 Decide whether the change is ready to close
Make the completion decision against explicit conditions:

both named changes pass strict artifact validation;
every accepted scenario maps to implementation and appropriate test evidence;
the reviewed OpenAPI document matches the client used by the frontend;
the configured backend, frontend, and cross-repository checks pass;
checked tasks correspond to completed work rather than planned or partial work; and
the team has repaired unresolved warnings or recorded them as accepted limits.

Each OpenSpec root can archive its own change. For one user-facing feature, keep both changes active until the product path passes. If the repositories must close at different times, record that decision and link their completion states.






WarningA checked task is not test evidence



A checkbox records workflow state, not test evidence. Keep the command and result available where the team reviews completion.

11.7 Inspect what archival preserved
An archive earns its place by making the decision recoverable. Open the dated directory and check for the material a future reviewer needs:

the proposal explaining the reason and scope;
delta specifications containing the reviewed scenarios;
the design decisions and boundaries used during implementation;
the completed task history;
the Jira issue and cross-repository identifier; and
any repository convention that points to test, pull request, or release evidence.

Keep current behavior in the main specs and change history in the archive. Packing discarded options into the main spec makes current behavior harder to read. Treating the archive as a moved folder makes past decisions harder to recover.
After both archive operations, compare their identifiers and resulting main specs. Keep API behavior in the backend spec and user-visible interaction in the frontend spec. Use the shared identifier to connect them without duplicating either specification.

11.11 Verification checkpoint
Accept the archive commits only when:

both changes pass strict artifact validation;
each accepted scenario points to implementation and reproducible evidence;
the generated frontend client reflects the reviewed backend contract;
the cross-repository path preserves the distinctions required by both deltas;
the updated main specs describe the behavior that passed verification;
the archives preserve the proposal, decisions, tasks, and linking identifiers; and
a fresh session can recover current behavior and change history without the old transcript.

Keep any unmet condition visible as an open item rather than hiding it behind an archive date.

12.2 Why brownfield work changes the question
Greenfield design often begins with “What should this system do?” Brownfield work tends to begin with a less comfortable question: “What does this system already do that users or other software might depend on?”
The answer may be scattered across several sources:

accepted OpenSpec requirements;
controller and service behavior;
an OpenAPI document consumed by another repository;
generated client code;
frontend validation and visible states; and
tests that preserve cases no one thought to document elsewhere.

Do not give those sources equal authority by default. Start with accepted specifications and reviewed contracts. Use code and tests to find dependencies, contradictions, and behavior that may need a decision. Then let the delta describe the requested change without claiming to explain the full history of truck search.






WarningExisting code is evidence, not automatic intent



A current behavior may be intentional, accidental, or obsolete. Treat repository exploration as evidence for review rather than silently converting every code path into a requirement.

12.11 Hands-on exercise
Use the two lab repositories to propose, apply, verify, and archive VIN search. Keep a compact change record that lets another reviewer follow the work without replaying the chat:
Product change: add-vin-search
Backend change: add-vin-search-api
Frontend change: add-vin-search-ui
Preserved capability: unit-number-search
Contract handoff: &lt;OpenAPI artifact or commit&gt;
Focused VIN evidence: &lt;commands and results&gt;
Unit-number regression evidence: &lt;commands and results&gt;
Cross-repository evidence: &lt;browser test or recorded run&gt;
Open questions: &lt;owners and blocking status&gt;
Before applying either change, ask another reviewer to trace one VIN scenario from proposal to task and one unit-number scenario from accepted specification to regression evidence. Any broken trace would suggest that the change is not ready for implementation.

13.11 Hands-on exercise
Use the two lab repositories to propose, apply, verify, and archive the revised unit-number rule. Keep a compact record that another reviewer could follow without replaying the Copilot chat:
Product change: reduce-unit-number-limit
Accepted requirement: Search by unit number
Old rule: &lt;exact accepted wording&gt;
Reviewed replacement: &lt;exact revised wording&gt;
Preserved scenarios: &lt;scenario names&gt;
Open questions: &lt;owners and blocking status&gt;
Backend evidence: &lt;commands and results&gt;
Contract evidence: &lt;OpenAPI and generated-client diffs&gt;
Frontend evidence: &lt;commands and results&gt;
Post-archive search: &lt;obsolete assumptions found or none&gt;
Pause when exploration exposes an unanswered product decision. Revise the delta after that decision receives review, then resume implementation from the updated scenarios. A code-only choice or a corrective chat message would leave the next agent with the same ambiguity.

14.11 Verification checkpoint
Before closing the change, ask whether a developer with no chat history could recover:

why Active Status joined the existing change;
which status values the contract accepts;
how two populated filters combine;
what each empty-selection case means;
which earlier work remained valid and which tasks reopened;
which tests provide evidence for every revised scenario.

Leave the change active if any answer depends on an old conversation or accidental implementation behavior. Archive only after the artifacts provide those answers and focused tests support the revised scenarios.

B.2 GitHub Copilot Commands and OpenSpec Skills
OpenSpec names each workflow by its Agent Skill, while the command a developer types depends on the agent harness running it. This section covers GitHub Copilot inside VS Code, IntelliJ IDEA, and GitHub Copilot CLI. VS Code and IntelliJ IDEA use GitHub Copilot’s prompt-file integration. Because GitHub Copilot CLI does not read .github/prompts/, the installed skills are the appropriate option there.1
The appropriate integration can install the relevant files when you initialize the repository, for example with openspec init --tools github-copilot.2 The table uses the current OPSX command form for the Copilot extensions and the Agent Skill form for Copilot CLI. OpenSpec documents the command pattern using the apply workflow; the other workflows appear to follow the same host-specific naming pattern.3










OpenSpec action
When to use it
VS Code
IntelliJ IDEA
GitHub Copilot CLI




explore
Investigate an idea, existing behavior, or an unclear requirement before creating a change proposal. It is a thinking step and does not implement code.
/opsx-explore &lt;Topic&gt;
/opsx-explore &lt;Topic&gt;
/openspec-explore &lt;Topic&gt;


propose
Turn the agreed problem into a change proposal and generate its planning artifacts.
/opsx-propose &lt;change-name&gt;
/opsx-propose &lt;change-name&gt;
/openspec-propose &lt;change-name&gt;


apply
Implement the tasks in an existing proposal after reviewing its requirements and design.
/opsx-apply &lt;change-name&gt;
/opsx-apply &lt;change-name&gt;
/openspec-apply-change &lt;change-name&gt;


sync
Merge implemented delta requirements into openspec/specs/ while leaving the change proposal open. Use this when the specification is ready to become the current baseline but the change still needs follow-up work.
/opsx-sync &lt;change-name&gt;
/opsx-sync &lt;change-name&gt;
/openspec-sync-specs &lt;change-name&gt;


archive
Complete a finished change. This merges its deltas into the main specs and moves the change folder into openspec/changes/archive/.
/opsx-archive &lt;change-name&gt;
/opsx-archive &lt;change-name&gt;
/openspec-archive-change &lt;change-name&gt;




B.2.1 Using the matrix
The VS Code and IntelliJ IDEA entries match because both use the GitHub Copilot IDE integration. OpenSpec generates prompt files under .github/prompts/, which the Copilot extension can expose as slash commands in both editors.4
GitHub Copilot CLI uses the skill spelling. For example, the CLI equivalent of /opsx-apply pets-search would be /openspec-apply-change pets-search. The IDE command is not expected to resolve in the CLI because the CLI does not read .github/prompts/.5
You might use the rows as a working loop rather than as mandatory ceremony for every small edit. Explore can help when the problem or existing behavior is unclear. Propose fits once the intended change is understood well enough to record. Apply is available when the proposal is ready to execute. Sync can update the main specification while the change remains open. Archive generally follows verification and completion, when the change is ready to leave the active-work area and its deltas can become part of the lasting specs.6
The terminal CLI is a separate interface. Commands such as openspec init and openspec validate can manage the project and its artifacts from a shell. Slash commands and skills ask an AI coding tool to carry out a workflow using those artifacts. If a workflow is unavailable, it may help to check that the repository is initialized and valid and that the selected host can see the files it expects.
